== Comforts Following Convictions in a Certain Order

VIII. Nothing can be determined with certainty about the nature of the affections from this, that comforts and joys seem to follow awakenings and convictions of conscience in a #emph[certain order.]

Many persons seem to be prejudiced against affections and experiences that come in the way many divines have much insisted on: first, such awakenings, fears and fearful apprehensions, followed by such legal humblings in a sense of total sinfulness and helplessness, and then such and such light and comfort. They look on all such schemes, laying down such methods and steps, as devised by men. And particularly if high affections of joy follow great distress and terror, many make that an argument against those affections. But such prejudices and objections have neither reason nor Scripture behind them. Surely it cannot be unreasonable to suppose that before God delivers persons from a state of sin and of exposure to eternal destruction, he should give them some considerable sense of the evil he delivers them from. He does it so that they may be delivered in a way they feel, and understand their own salvation, and know something of what God does for them. Men who are saved are in two exceedingly different states, first a state of condemnation, and then a state of justification and blessedness. And God, in the work of the salvation of mankind, deals with them in a way suited to their intelligent, rational nature. So it seems reasonable, and agreeable to God’s wisdom, that men who are saved should be in these two states in a way they feel. First, they should feel themselves to be in a state of condemnation, and so in a state of woeful calamity and dreadful misery, and afterwards feel themselves to be in a state of deliverance and happiness. They should first have a sense of their absolute, extreme need, and afterwards of Christ’s sufficiency and God’s mercy through him.

And the Scripture shows abundantly that it is God’s manner of dealing with men to #emph[lead them into the wilderness before he speaks tenderly to them.] It is his manner to order things so that they will be brought into distress, and made to see their own helplessness and their absolute dependence on his power and grace, before he appears to work any great deliverance for them. It is then that God usually #emph[has compassion on his professing people, when their strength is gone and no one remains,] and when they are brought to see that their false gods cannot help them, and that the rock in which they trusted is vain (Deut 32:36–37). Before God delivered the children of #emph[Israel] out of #emph[Egypt,] they were prepared for it by being made to #emph[see that they were in trouble,] and #emph[to cry out to God because of their hard bondage] (Exod 2:23; 5:19). And before God worked that great deliverance for them at the Red Sea, they were brought into great distress. #emph[The wilderness had shut them in;] they could not turn to the right hand or the left; the #emph[Red Sea] was before them and the great army of #emph[Egypt] behind. They were brought to see that they could do nothing to help themselves, and that if God did not help them, they would be swallowed up immediately. And then God appeared and turned their cries into songs. So before they were brought to their rest, and to enjoy the milk and honey of #emph[Canaan,] God #emph[led them through the vast and terrifying wilderness, to humble them and teach them what was in their heart, so that in the end he might do them good] (Deut 8:2, 16). The woman who had suffered from bleeding for twelve years was not delivered until she had first #emph[spent all her money on earthly physicians, and no one was able to heal her.] So she was left helpless, having no more money to spend. And then she came to the great Physician, without money and without price, and was healed by him (Luke 8:43–44). Before Christ would answer the request of the woman of #emph[Canaan,] he first seemed to refuse her utterly, and humbled her, and brought her to own herself worthy to be called a dog. And then he showed her mercy and received her as a dear child (Matt 15:22 and following). The apostle #emph[Paul,] before a remarkable deliverance, was #emph[under a burden far beyond his ability to endure, so that he despaired even of life, and had the sentence of death in himself, so that he would not trust in himself but in God, who raises the dead] (2 Cor 1:8–10). There was first a great storm, and the boat was covered by the waves and just ready to sink, and the disciples were brought to cry to Jesus, “Lord, save us! We are perishing!” And then the winds and the sea were rebuked, and there was a great calm (Matt 8:24–26). The leper, before he is cleansed, must have his mouth stopped by a covering on his upper lip, and was to acknowledge his great misery and utter uncleanness by tearing his clothes and crying, “Unclean, unclean!” (Lev 13:45). And backsliding #emph[Israel,] before God heals them, are brought to #emph[acknowledge that they have sinned and have not obeyed the voice of the LORD,] and to see that #emph[they lie down in their shame and their disgrace covers them,] and #emph[that salvation is hoped for in vain from the hills and from the multitude of mountains,] and that God alone can save them (Jer 3:23–25). #emph[Joseph,] who was sold by his brothers and in that was a type of Christ, brings his brothers into great perplexity and distress. He brings them to reflect on their sin and to say, “We are truly guilty,” and at last to give themselves up entirely into his hands as slaves. And then he reveals himself to them as their brother and their savior.

And if we consider the extraordinary manifestations God made of himself to the saints of old, we will find that he commonly manifested himself first in a way that was terrifying, and then by things that were comforting. So it was with #emph[Abraham.] First #emph[great terror and darkness overwhelmed him,] and then God revealed himself to him in sweet promises (Gen 15:12–13). So it was with #emph[Moses] at Mount #emph[Sinai.] First God appeared to him in all the terrors of his dreadful majesty, so that #emph[Moses] said, “I am trembling with fear.” And then he made all his goodness pass before him and proclaimed his name, #emph[the LORD God, compassionate and gracious,] and so on. So it was with #emph[Elijah.] First there is a stormy wind, and an earthquake, and a devouring fire, and then a still, small, sweet voice (1 Kgs 19). So it was with #emph[Daniel.] He first saw Christ’s face like lightning, which terrified him and made him faint away. And then he is strengthened and refreshed with comforting words such as these: “#emph[Daniel,] you are a man who is highly precious” (Dan 10). So it was with the apostle #emph[John] (Rev 1). And a likeness can be observed between God’s dispensations and the deliverances he works for his people, and the manifestations he makes of himself to them, both ordinary and extraordinary.

But there are many things in Scripture which show more directly that this is God’s ordinary manner in working salvation for the souls of men, and in the manifestations God makes of himself and of his mercy in Christ in the ordinary works of his grace on the hearts of sinners. The servant who owed his prince ten thousand talents is first held to his debt. The king pronounces sentence of condemnation on him and commands that he, his wife and his children be sold, and payment be made. In this way he humbles him and brings him to own that the whole debt is just, and then he forgives him all of it. The prodigal son spends all he has, and is brought to see himself in extreme circumstances, and to humble himself and own his unworthiness, before he is relieved and feasted by his father (#emph[Luke] 15). Old, deep-rooted wounds must be probed to the bottom in order to be healed. And the Scripture compares sin, the wound of the soul, to this, and speaks of healing this wound without probing it in this way as vain and deceitful (Jer 8:11). Christ, in the work of his grace on the hearts of men, is compared to rain on the mown grass, grass that has been cut down with a scythe (Ps 72:6). This represents his refreshing, comforting influences on the wounded spirit. Our first parents, after they had sinned, were first terrified with God’s majesty and justice, and had their sin with its aggravations set before them by their judge, before they were relieved by the promise of the seed of the woman. Christians are spoken of as those #emph[who have fled for refuge to lay hold of the hope set before them] (Heb 6:18). This picture implies great fear and a sense of danger going before. To the same purpose, Christ is called “a shelter from the wind, a refuge from the storm, like streams of water in a dry land, like the shadow of a great rock in an arid land” (Isa 32, at the beginning). And it seems to be the natural meaning of the word #emph[gospel,] good news, that it is news of deliverance and salvation after great fear and distress. There is every reason to suppose that God deals with particular believers as he dealt with his church. He first made her hear his voice in the law, with terrible thunders and lightnings, and kept her under that schoolmaster to prepare her for Christ. And then he comforted her with the joyful sound of the gospel from Mount #emph[Zion.] So likewise #emph[John the Baptist] came to prepare the way for Christ, and to prepare men’s hearts to receive him. He did it by showing them their sins, and by bringing the self-righteous #emph[Jews] off from their own righteousness. He told them that they were a #emph[brood of vipers,] and showed them their danger of #emph[the coming wrath,] telling them that #emph[the axe lies ready at the root of the trees,] and so on.

And it is indeed God’s manner, as I think the foregoing considerations show beyond doubt, to give men a considerable sense of the greatness and dreadfulness of their sin and misery, and of their extreme wretchedness because of them, before he gives them the comfort of a deliverance from them. If so, surely it is not unreasonable to suppose that persons, often at least, should have great distress and terrible apprehensions of mind while under these views. This is especially so if we consider what these evils are which they see. They are no other than great and manifold sins against the infinite majesty of the great Jehovah, and the suffering of the fierceness of his wrath to all eternity. And it is still more so when we have many plain instances in Scripture of persons who have actually been brought into extreme distress by such convictions before they received saving consolations. There was the multitude at #emph[Jerusalem,] who were “cut to the heart and asked Peter and the other apostles, ‘Brothers, what shall we do?’” And there was the apostle #emph[Paul,] who #emph[trembled and was astonished] before he was comforted. And there was the #emph[jailer:] “Calling for lights, the jailer rushed in and fell trembling before Paul and Silas … ‘Sirs, what must I do to be saved?’”

From these things it appears very unreasonable in professing Christians to make this an objection against the truth and spiritual nature of the comforting and joyful affections anyone has, that they follow such fearful apprehensions and distresses as have been mentioned.

And on the other hand, it is no evidence that comforts and joys are right because they come after great terrors and overwhelming fears of hell.#footnote[Mr. #emph[Shepard] speaks of men being cast down as low as hell by sorrow, and lying under chains, quaking in apprehension of terror to come, and then raised up to heaven in joy, not able to live; and yet not torn away from lust; and such are objects of pity now, and are likely to be the objects of terror at the great day. #emph[Parable of the ten virgins,] P. 1. P. 125.] Some persons seem to lay great weight on this. They regard great terrors as evidence of a great work of the law done on the heart, preparing the way well for solid comfort. They do not consider that terror and conviction of conscience are different things. For though convictions of conscience often cause terror, they do not consist in it, and terrors often arise from other causes. Convictions of conscience through the influences of God’s Spirit consist in conviction of the sinfulness of heart and practice, and of the dreadfulness of sin as committed against a God of terrible majesty, infinite holiness and hatred of sin, and strict justice in punishing it. But there are some persons who have frightening apprehensions of hell, a dreadful pit ready to swallow them up, flames just ready to take hold of them, and devils around them ready to seize them, and who at the same time seem to have very little proper enlightening of conscience, really convincing them of their sinfulness of heart and life. The devil, if permitted, can terrify men as well as the Spirit of God can. It is a work natural to him, and he has many ways of doing it in a manner that tends to no good. He may frighten persons exceedingly by impressing on them many external images and ideas: a frowning face, a drawn sword, black clouds of vengeance, words of a fearful doom pronounced,#footnote[The way of the Spirit’s working, when he does convince men, is by enlightening natural conscience. The Spirit does not work by giving a testimony, but by assisting natural conscience to do its work. Natural conscience is the instrument in the hand of God to accuse, condemn, terrify, and urge to duty. The Spirit of God leads men to consider their danger and makes them affected with it: “The spirit of a man is the lamp of the LORD, searching out his inmost being” (Prov 20:27). Stoddard’s #emph[Guide to Christ,] p. 44.] hell gaping, devils coming, and the like. He does it not to convince persons of things that are true and revealed in the word of God, but to lead them to vain and groundless conclusions: that their day is past, that they are reprobate, that God cannot be appeased, that he has resolved to cut them off immediately, #emph[and so on.]

And the terrors some persons have are very much owing to their particular constitution and temperament. Nothing is plainer than that some persons are of such a temperament and frame that their imaginations are more strongly impressed than others’ with everything they are affected with. And the impression on the imagination acts back on the affection and raises it still higher. And so affection and imagination act on one another in turn, until their affection is raised to a vast height, and the person is swallowed up and loses all possession of himself.#footnote[The famous Mr. #emph[Perkins] distinguishes between #emph[those sorrows that come through convictions of conscience, and melancholy passions rising only from mere imaginations strongly conceived in the brain, which, he says, usually come on suddenly, like lightning into a house.] Vol. 1. Of his works, p. 385.]

And some speak of a great sight they have of their wickedness who, when the matter comes to be well examined and thoroughly weighed, are really found to have little or no conviction of conscience. They tell of a dreadfully hard heart, and how their heart lies like a stone, when in truth they have none of those things in their minds or thoughts in which the hardness of men’s hearts really consists. They tell of a dreadful load and sink of sin, a heap of black and loathsome filth within them, when, if the matter is carefully inquired into, they have nothing in view in which the corruption of nature truly consists. Nor do they have any thought of any particular thing in which their hearts are sinfully defective, or fall short of what ought to be in them, or any exercises of corruption in them at all. And many also think they have great convictions of their actual sins who truly have none. They tell how their sins are set in order before them; they see them standing around them in a row with a dreadful, frightening appearance. And yet in reality not so much as one of the sins they have been guilty of in the course of their lives comes into view in such a way that they are affected with its aggravations.

And if persons have had great terrors which really have been from the awakening and convincing influences of the Spirit of God, it does not follow from this that their terrors must necessarily end in true comfort. The unmortified corruption of the heart may quench the Spirit of God (after he has been striving) by leading men to presumptuous and self-exalting hopes and joys, as well as in other ways. Not every woman who is really in labor brings forth a real child. It may be a monstrous birth, without anything of the form or properties of human nature belonging to it. #emph[Pharaoh’s] chief baker, after he had lain in the dungeon with #emph[Joseph,] had a vision that raised his hopes, and he was lifted up out of the dungeon as well as the chief cupbearer, but it was to be hanged.

But suppose comforts and joys not only come after great terrors and awakenings, but there is an appearance of #emph[such] preparatory convictions and humiliations, brought about very distinctly by #emph[such] steps and in #emph[such] a method as has often been observed in true converts. Even this is no certain sign that the light and comforts which follow are true and saving. And this is for the following reasons.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[First,] as the devil can counterfeit all the saving workings and graces of the Spirit of God, so he can counterfeit the workings that are preparatory to grace. Satan can counterfeit those effects of God’s Spirit which are special, divine and sanctifying, so that there will be a very great resemblance in everything others can observe. If so, he may much more easily imitate those works of God’s Spirit which are common, which men experience while they are still Satan’s own children. These works are in no way so far above him as the others. There are no works of God so high and divine, so far above the powers of nature and out of the reach of the power of all creatures, as those works of his Spirit by which he forms the creature in his own image and makes it a partaker of the divine nature. But if the devil can be the author of such resemblances of these as have been spoken of, without doubt he may be the author of resemblances of those that are of an infinitely lower kind. And it is abundantly evident in fact that there are false humiliations and false submissions as well as false comforts.#footnote[The venerable #emph[Stoddard] observes, “A man may say that now he can justify God however he deals with him, and not be brought off from his own righteousness. And some men do justify God from a partial conviction of the righteousness of their condemnation. Conscience takes notice of their sinfulness and tells them that they may be righteously damned, as #emph[Pharaoh] justified God (Exod 9:27). And they give some kind of consent to it, but many times it does not continue. They have only a pang on them, which usually dies away after a little time.” #emph[Guide to Christ,] p. 71.] How far #emph[Saul] was brought, though a very wicked man and of a haughty spirit! He, though a great king, was brought in conviction of his sin as it were to fall down, all in tears, weeping aloud, before #emph[David,] his own subject (and one he had mortally hated for a long time and openly treated as an enemy), and to condemn himself before him, crying out, “You are more righteous than I, for you have rewarded me with good, though I have rewarded you with evil.” And at another time he said, “I have sinned … I have played the fool and have committed a grave error!” (1 Sam 24:16–17; 26:21). And yet #emph[Saul] seems then to have had very little of the influences of the Spirit of God, for it was after God’s Spirit had departed from him and given him up, and an evil spirit from the LORD troubled him. And if this proud monarch, in a pang of affection, was brought to humble himself so low before a subject he hated, and to whom he still remained an enemy, there may doubtless be appearances of great conviction and humiliation before God in men while they still remain his enemies, and even though they remain so to the end. In men who are terrified through fears of hell there is often a great appearance of their being brought off from their own righteousness, when they are not brought off from it in all ways, though they are in many of the ways that are plainer and more visible. They have only exchanged some ways of trusting in their own righteousness for others that are more secret and subtle. Often a great degree of discouragement about many things they used to depend on is taken for humiliation. And that is called a submission to God which is no absolute submission, but has some secret bargain in it that is hard to detect.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] if the workings and effects of the Spirit of God in the convictions and comforts of true converts may be counterfeited, then their order may be imitated. If Satan can imitate the things themselves, he may easily put them one after another in such a certain order. If the devil can make A, B and C, it is as easy for him to put A first, B next and C after, as to arrange them in the opposite order. The nature of divine things is harder for the devil to imitate than their order. He cannot imitate divine workings #emph[exactly] in their nature, though his counterfeits may be very much like them in outward appearance. But he can imitate their order exactly. When counterfeits are made, no divine power is needed to place one of them first and another last. And therefore no order or method of workings and experiences is any certain sign of their divinity. Only that which Satan cannot do, and which cannot possibly be brought about by any power short of divine, is to be trusted as a certain evidence of grace.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Thirdly,] we have no certain rule to determine how far God’s own Spirit may go in workings and convictions which in themselves are not spiritual and saving, while the person who experiences them is never converted, but falls short of salvation at last. There is no necessary connection in the nature of things between anything a natural man may experience while in a state of nature and the saving grace of God’s Spirit. And if there is no connection in the nature of things, then there can be no known and certain connection at all, except by divine revelation. But there is no revealed certain connection between a state of salvation and anything a natural man can experience before he believes in Christ. God has revealed no certain connection between salvation and any qualifications in men except grace and its fruits. And therefore we do not find any legal convictions, or comforts following those legal convictions in any certain method or order, ever once mentioned in the Scripture as certain signs of grace or as things peculiar to the saints, though we do find gracious workings and effects themselves mentioned in this way thousands of times. This should be enough for Christians who are willing to have the word of God, rather than their own philosophy and experiences and conjectures, as their sufficient and sure guide in things of this nature.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Fourthly,] experience strongly confirms that persons seeming to have convictions and comforts following one another in the method and order often observed in true converts is no certain sign of grace.#footnote[Mr. #emph[Stoddard,] who had much experience of things of this nature, observed long ago that converted and unconverted men cannot be distinguished with certainty by the account they give of their experience, the same #emph[account of experiences being common to both;] and #emph[that many persons have given a fair account of a work of conversion, and have behaved well in the eyes of the world for several years, but have not proved well at last.] Appeal to the learned, p. 75, and 76.] I appeal to all the ministers in this land who have had much occasion to deal with souls in the recent extraordinary season. Have there not been many who do not prove well, who have given a fair account of their experiences and have seemed to be converted according to rule, #emph[that is,] with convictions and affections following one another distinctly and exactly in the order and method that has ordinarily been insisted on as the order of the workings of the Spirit of God in conversion?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[And as seeming to have this distinctness of steps and method is no certain sign that a person is converted, so being without it is no evidence that a person is not converted. It might be demonstrated on Scripture principles that a sinner who is not convinced of his sin and misery, of his own emptiness and helplessness, and of his just desert of eternal condemnation, cannot be brought to receive Christ heartily as his Savior, and that therefore such convictions must be #emph[implied] in some way in what is worked in his soul. Yet nothing proves it necessary that all the things which are implied or presupposed in an act of faith in Christ must be worked plainly and distinctly in the soul, in so many successive and separate works of the Spirit, each one plain and evident, in all who are truly converted. On the contrary, as Mr. #emph[Shepard] observes, the change made in a saint at the first work is at times like a confused chaos, so that the saints do not know what to make of it. The manner of the Spirit’s proceeding in those who are born of the Spirit is very often exceedingly mysterious and unsearchable. We hear the sound of it, as it were, and its effect can be discerned, but no man can tell where it came from or where it went. And it is often as difficult to know the way of the Spirit in the new birth as in the first birth: “As you do not know the way of the Spirit, or how the bones are formed in a mother’s womb, so you cannot understand the work of God, the Maker of all things” (Eccl 11:5). The begetting of a principle of grace in the soul seems in Scripture to be compared to the conceiving of Christ in the womb (Gal 4:19). And therefore the church is called Christ’s mother (Song 3:11), and so is every particular believer (Matt 12:49–50). And the conception of Christ in the womb of the blessed virgin by the power of the Holy Spirit seems to be an intended likeness of the conception of Christ in the soul of a believer by the power of the same Holy Spirit. And we do not know the way of the Spirit, or how the bones grow, either in the womb or in the heart that conceives this holy child. The new creature may use the language of Psalm 139:14–15: “I am fearfully and wonderfully made. Marvelous are Your works, and I know this very well. My frame was not hidden from You when I was made in secret.” Of the generation of Christ, both in his person and in the hearts of his people, it may be said, as in Isaiah 53:8, “Who can declare His generation?” We do not know the works of God, the Maker of all things. “It is the glory of God to conceal a matter” (Prov 25:2), and to have #emph[his path as it were in the mighty waters, so that his footprints may not be known,] and especially in the works of his Spirit on the hearts of men, which are the highest and chief of his works. And therefore it is said in Isa 40:13, “Who has directed the Spirit of the LORD, or informed Him as His counselor?” It is to be feared that some have gone too far toward directing the Spirit of the LORD, marking out his footsteps for him and limiting him to certain steps and methods. Experience plainly shows that in some of the best of Christians God’s Spirit is unsearchable and untraceable in the method of his workings in their conversion. Nor does the Spirit of God proceed discernibly in the steps of a particular established scheme half as often as is imagined. A scheme of what is necessary, according to a rule already received and established by common opinion, has a vast influence (though to many a very imperceptible one) in forming persons’ notions of the steps and method of their own experiences. I know very well what their way is, for I have had much opportunity to observe it. Very often at first their experiences appear like a confused #emph[chaos,] as Mr. #emph[Shepard] puts it. But then the parts of their experience are picked out which look most like the particular steps that are insisted on. These are dwelt on in their thoughts, and these are told of from time to time in the account they give. These parts grow brighter and brighter in their view, and others, being neglected, grow more and more obscure. And what they have experienced is imperceptibly stretched to bring it all into exact conformity with the scheme that is established. And it becomes natural for ministers who have to deal with them and guide them, who insist on distinctness and clearness of method, to do the same. But even so, there has been so much to be seen of the workings of the Spirit of God lately that those who have had much to do with souls, and are not blinded with a sevenfold veil of prejudice, must know this: the Spirit is so exceedingly varied in his manner of working that in many cases it is impossible to trace him or find out his way.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[What we have chiefly to do with, in our inquiries into our own state or the directions we give to others, is the #emph[nature] of the effect God has brought about in the soul. As to the steps the Spirit of God took to bring that effect about, we may leave them to him. We are often expressly told in Scripture to test ourselves by the #emph[nature] of the fruits of the Spirit, but nowhere by the Spirit’s #emph[method] of producing them.#footnote[Mr. #emph[Shepard,] speaking of the soul’s embracing Christ, says, “As a child cannot tell how its soul comes into it, nor perhaps when, but afterwards sees and feels that life, so that he would be as bad as a beast who should deny an immortal soul, so it is here.” Parable of the ten virgins, Part II. P. 171. “If the man does not know the time of his conversion, or first embracing Christ, the minister may not draw any absolute conclusion from that, that he is not godly.” #emph[Stoddard’s] Guide to Christ, p. 83. “Do not think there is no compunction or sense of sin worked in the soul because you cannot discern and feel it so clearly, nor the time of its working and first beginning. I have known many who have come with their complaints that they #emph[were never humbled, they never felt it so;] yet there it has been, and many times they have seen it by other spectacles and blessed God for it.” #emph[Shepard’s] sound believer, p. 38. The recent impression in #emph[Boston.]] Many greatly err in their notions of a clear work of conversion. They call that a clear work where the successive steps of influence and the method of experience are clear. But in truth the clearest work is not where the order of #emph[doing] is clearest, but where the spiritual and divine nature of the work #emph[done,] and the effect #emph[produced,] is clearest.]]
